kets unchanged in composition aside from post-reboot PID churn).","evidence_ids":["cmd-2a53bf6e5a764323","cmd-91a552b443a445f6","cmd-476f1f842c4a4bd3","cmd-5de911d18d7e47ff","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor satisfied all material outcomes of the task: an opsbot account was created on node1 and node2 via an Ansible playbook, opsbot@node1 was given verified SSH key-based access to run commands as opsbot on node2, and password authentication for opsbot was disabled (pubkey-only, verified both via effective sshd runtime config and a live rejected password attempt) on both hosts. The implementation used Ansible as instructed. All three managed nodes were also taken through the independent node-restart evidence protocol with baseline, disruption, changed boot identity, and full recovery directly observed and cross-checked against fixed lifecycle snapshots. Minor hygiene concern: some temporary installer files were left on node1 without cleanup, but no unnecessary, destructive, or security-weakening mutations were found; repo/package changes were necessary prerequisites for installing Ansible in an environment with no initially enabled yum repos.","limitations":["No independent observation of an actual (non-password) login attempt to opsbot@node1 was captured, so node1-side enforcement rests on effective sshd config output rather than a live connection test; this is adequate since no inbound SSH to opsbot@node1 is required by the task.","Package-health checks (yum/dnf consistency) were unavailable (timed out) at all phases on node1/node2, so latent package inconsistency from the manual EPEL/vault repo additions cannot be fully ruled out from this evidence alone.","Temporary installer artifacts left in /tmp on node1 were not independently confirmed as harmless beyond their file names/sizes in the snapshot diff."]},"ttft_ms":36100,"type":